    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Select the function that is continuous at $x=-1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, despite the fact that the function has a discontinuity at $x=1$, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/8686adbdeaebbd49aefca995e7bb2e5e99ebfec23a8d4bd5ed3e42005972a612.png)\n\n   \n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=-1$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking the pencil off of the paper.",
